tretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\frac{sin⁡(-x)}{2}.$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{sin⁡(-x)}{2}=-\\frac{sin⁡(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-\\frac{sin⁡(x)}{2}.$ However, $y=-\\frac{sin⁡(x)}{2}$ is not among the given options." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-66202/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85090bdf-4f1b-5f79-a751-48521ad98895"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66202"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6777"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933758/assets/c27ff316601ece28946ef45711690d6aabca92a4c35f07b0b1cc1c0982fb5d8e.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **up** to give $y=-cos⁡x+1$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-cos⁡x+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-118450/selection/367801e43af8e42d8d39bf5ec0a99a45ebfe785f2a5b3e449e1aef17521f85ee" :answer/id #uuid "f8625dcd-2689-5277-8fdd-ea789c0707ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-sin⁡x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118450/selection/75e7da2b4230aaee8368e61e69805f07fff5f1f9139994978750d840997b72be" :answer/id #uuid "ff8b23fd-ee38-5ab3-9c80-a890ddea2e2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(1-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118450/selection/994e2ec5b4da3aecf601eb5f82be17c0edf17148c911e91ea2ade07de4e29080" :answer/id #uuid "8e52d71a-bdfd-5069-b53f-44a27f0aad41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answe